Hecks.adapter_family "exec" do
  # DEPRECATED (adapters-as-bluebook, plan: structured-pondering-pie).
  # The bespoke `resolve_exec_adapters` runtime arm that drove this
  # family is RETIRED. Every former `:exec` binding (Inbox.Check,
  # ProcessMacrophage.Sweep/Heal, the fibroblast sweep) is now an
  # ordinary bluebook policy firing `Primitive::Process.Spawn` — the
  # generic process-spawn primitive whose only imperative leaf is the
  # spawn syscall (runtime/exec_dispatcher.rs, reached via
  # resolve_primitive_spawn). `adapter :exec, ...` blocks no longer do
  # anything at runtime ; this family declaration is kept only as a
  # historical descriptor + the framework-registry index entry. New
  # side-effects should be expressed as a policy → Primitive::Process.Spawn
  # → Cascade.RecordResult, NOT as an :exec adapter.
  #
  # ── original vision (historical) ──
  # The :exec adapter family — bind one aggregate.command dispatch to
  # one local executable, run it on dispatch, cascade its outcome. The
  # sibling of :claude_tool (fixed harness tool primitives) and
  # :web_tool (one HTTP fetch). :exec runs an ARBITRARY local program
  # named by the adapter instance's :exec field — the general case the
  # others are specializations of.
  #
  # Why :exec and NOT :shell : the Rust hecksagon parser already routes
  # `adapter :shell` to a pre-existing legacy ShellAdapter (run a shell
  # command, with command/args/ok_exit) — different semantics. Reusing
  # :shell would collide and the binding would be mis-parsed as a
  # legacy shell-command adapter. The hecksagon-parity gate surfaced
  # this. :exec is a clean, unclaimed keyword (i629 named it as the
  # alternative). The legacy :shell keeps parse_shell_adapter ; :exec
  # gets its own route.
  #
  # Why it exists (i629) : Inbox.Check is locked to a multi-step poll
  # (OAuth refresh + Gmail historyId delta + per-thread card + draft).
  # No prior family could run a multi-step local executor from a
  # dispatch, so the poll shipped as a transitional standalone Procfile
  # member (inboxpoll). This family lets `storehouse loop ...
  # Inbox::Inbox.Check` BE the poll — the dispatch is the fetch, the
  # transitional member retires, "no new process" becomes literally true.
  #
  # ── The principle the binding sits on ──
  #   "the domain composes the call ; the adapter runs it"
  # The bluebook (Inbox.Check, pure composition) names the contract ;
  # the :exec adapter (execution) is the only impure layer. Same
  # IO-monad shape as :claude_tool / :web_tool.

  vision "Bind one aggregate.command to one local executable. On
          dispatch the kernel resolves the matching :exec adapter
          (one with command: \"<Agg>.<Cmd>\"), runs its :exec program
          to completion, captures stdout / stderr / exit code, caps
          the captured output, and routes the result into
          :result_into as a follow-on dispatch. One behavior_kind :
          perform_exec. No providers — the exec is the provider."

  fields  :name,         # symbolic adapter name in the consumer's hecksagon
          :command,      # "Inbox.Check" — the dispatched aggregate.command this adapter binds to
          :exec,         # "node bin/inbox_poll.mjs" — the local program run on dispatch (cwd = aggregates root parent)
          :result_into   # "Cascade.RecordResult" — follow-on dispatch carrying the outcome

  trigger_field :command
  response_field :result_into

  # One behavior kind : run the exec. Adding a new exec "operation" is
  # not a thing — the exec IS the operation. Deliberately minimal so
  # it cannot grow into a second tool surface.
  behaviors :perform_exec

  # No providers. :web_tool has :http_get / :duckduckgo because a fetch
  # has wire variants ; a local exec has none — the :exec string fully
  # determines the side effect. Empty providers is the fence that stops
  # :exec becoming a god-adapter.
  providers
end
